    "type": "assistant_message",
    "content": "All 19 checklist items have been executed and recorded — **17 PASS, 2 FAIL**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe core to-do functionality is solid: task creation with full field retention, editing, deletion, completion with count updates, active/completed view switching, both sort modes (including undated tasks sorting last), cycle-safe prerequisite selection, empty/whitespace title rejection, blocked-state styling, and both hover tooltips (dependency-chain popups on the dashed connectors and on the blocked warnings) all behaved as expected.\n\nTwo defects were found, both in dependency-state handling:\n\n**FT-3 — FAIL: blocked tasks are completely uneditable.**\nThe requirement is that a blocked task still allows description and priority changes while locking title and deadline. Instead, the edit button carries the HTML `disabled` attribute, so no edit form ever opens. A full scan of the blocked card's controls returned only `complete` (disabled), `edit` (disabled), `delete` (enabled) — no inline editing or alternative affordance exists. The implementation followed the literal \"edit button grayed out\" wording at the expense of the \"description and priority remain modifiable\" requirement.\n\n**CS-22 — FAIL: reopening a prerequisite creates an unrecoverable invalid state.**\nAfter completing task 4 and then reopening its prerequisite (task 1), task 4 remained in the completed view and completed count *while simultaneously* displaying `Prerequisite \"Review project proposal\" incomplete`. The system neither blocked the reopen nor reverted the dependent task. This is aggravated by an interaction with the FT-3 defect: because the task is now blocked, both its complete toggle and edit button are disabled, so the user cannot un-complete or edit it to escape the contradiction — only deletion remains, which loses the task entirely.\n\nA note on method: two checks needed care beyond the semantic snapshot. The sort and priority controls are Radix comboboxes rather than native `<select>`, so `browser_select_option` failed and I drove them via trigger-plus-option clicks. For IX-18, the dependency connectors are perfectly vertical SVG paths whose zero-width bounding box makes Playwright's `hover` refuse them as \"not visible\"; rather than calling that a failure, I used hit-testing (`elementFromPoint`) to confirm a real mouse genuinely lands on the 16px-wide hit path in the gaps between cards, then dispatched the hover there — the tooltip opened correctly, so the feature works and the obstacle was purely a tooling artifact."
  }
}
